      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "I’ll use the cycle-coloring formula, `(m - 1)^n + (-1)^n(m - 1)`, computed modulo `998244353`. Then I’ll run the supplied tests, check small cases by brute force, and publish the implementation.",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": []
        }
      ],
      "phase": "commentary"
    },
    {
      "type": "function_call",
      "id": "fc_01f5223ec8199076016ad811ae8fb08191bb2970c9ce35fb75",
      "call_id": "call_46e287c55c9b4e9a908a20695e9d3f57",
      "name": "bash",
      "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cat > /workspace/src/component.py <<'PY'\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Utilities for counting proper colorings of a labeled circle.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n_MODULUS = 998244353\\n\\n\\ndef count_valid_colorings(n: int, m: int) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Count assignments of ``m`` colors to a circle of ``n`` people.\\n\\n    Adjacent people, including the first and last, must have different\\n    colors. People are labeled, so rotations are counted separately.\\n    The result is returned modulo 998244353.\\n\\n    Args:\\n        n: Number of people, with 2 <= n <= 10**6.\\n        m: Number of available colors, with 2 <= m <= 10**6.\\n\\n    Returns:\\n        The number of valid assignments modulo 998244353.\\n\\n    The cycle coloring formula is (m - 1)**n + (-1)**n * (m - 1).\\n    It also covers n = 2, yielding m * (m - 1). Modular exponentiation\\n    computes the result in O(log n) time and O(1) auxiliary space.\\n\\n    >>> count_valid_colorings(3, 3)\\n    6\\n    >>> count_valid_colorings(4, 2)\\n    2\\n    >>> count_valid_colorings(987654, 456789)\\n    778634319\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    other_colors = m - 1\\n    correction = -other_colors if n % 2 else other_colors\\n    return (pow(other_colors, n, _MODULUS) + correction) % _MODULUS\\nPY\\ncd /workspace && python tests.py && python -m doctest -v src/component.py && python - <<'PY'\\nfrom itertools import product\\nfrom random import Random\\nfrom src.component import count_valid_colorings\\n\\nMOD = 998244353\\n\\n# Enumerate labeled assignments directly, including the n=2 boundary.\\nexhaustive_cases = 0\\nfor n in range(2, 9):\\n    for m in range(2, 5):\\n        expected = sum(\\n            all(colors[i] != colors[(i + 1) % n] for i in range(n))\\n            for colors in product(range(m), repeat=n)\\n        )\\n        assert count_valid_colorings(n, m) == expected % MOD, (n, m)\\n        exhaustive_cases += 1\\n\\n# Independent DP: fix the first color and track whether the last matches it.\\nrng = Random(625446)\\nfor _ in range(200):\\n    n = rng.randint(2, 2000)\\n    m = rng.randint(2, 10**6)\\n    same, different = 1, 0\\n    for _ in range(n - 1):\\n        same, different = different, (same * (m - 1) + different * (m - 2)) % MOD\\n    expected = (m * different) % MOD\\n    actual = count_valid_colorings(n, m)\\n    assert actual == expected, (n, m, actual, expected)\\n\\nfor n in (2, 3, 999999, 1000000):\\n    assert count_valid_colorings(n, 2) == (0 if n % 2 else 2)\\n\\nprint(f'Additional checks passed: {exhaustive_cases} exhaustive cases, 200 DP cases, 4 boundary cases.')\\nPY\"}",
      "status": "completed"
    }
